00:03:26.790 --> 00:03:30.400
is clearly really,
really important.

00:03:30.400 --> 00:03:32.970
And so we get to sort of how
do you think about storage

00:03:32.970 --> 00:03:35.820
technologies, and again, I
just want to kind of gently

00:03:35.820 --> 00:03:39.600
introduce us to energy storage
and we'll go into batteries.

00:03:39.600 --> 00:03:44.610
Now so the kind of plot that
I'm showing you here, talking

00:03:44.610 --> 00:03:47.100
about Ashby plots, right,
but this is actually

00:03:47.100 --> 00:03:47.830
a different kind.

00:03:47.830 --> 00:03:53.100
It's a special kind because it
has to do with energy storage,

00:03:53.100 --> 00:04:03.060
and there's a guy who in the
1960s, David Ragone, he first--

00:04:03.060 --> 00:04:04.350
And it's actually fascinating.

00:04:04.350 --> 00:04:07.710
He wrote a paper
in 1968, a review

00:04:07.710 --> 00:04:13.255
of batteries for electric
vehicles in 1968, right.

00:04:13.255 --> 00:04:14.880
And he's saying, how
do I put-- there's

00:04:14.880 --> 00:04:16.149
all these possibilities.

00:04:16.149 --> 00:04:18.390
We want to electrify our
transportation fleet,

00:04:18.390 --> 00:04:20.649
but which batteries
make the most sense?

00:04:20.649 --> 00:04:24.360
And so he decided to plot
them on these two axes,

00:04:24.360 --> 00:04:29.250
energy stored versus the power
and that's a very common way.

00:04:29.250 --> 00:04:31.860
These are called
Ragone plots now,

00:04:31.860 --> 00:04:35.940
so it's the energy versus power.

00:04:35.940 --> 00:04:39.493
And this is how you'll
see a lot of discussions.

00:04:39.493 --> 00:04:41.910
If you look online and you're
interested in energy storage

00:04:41.910 --> 00:04:44.850
as a field, you'll
see a lot of plots

00:04:44.850 --> 00:04:50.230
like this where you got, OK,
so energy is like watt hours,

00:04:50.230 --> 00:04:50.730
right.

00:04:50.730 --> 00:04:55.200
For example, this would
be like watts times hours

00:04:55.200 --> 00:04:58.710
and this would be
like the watts.

00:04:58.710 --> 00:05:03.323
So one of the nice things
about plots like this,

00:05:03.323 --> 00:05:04.740
mostly, it's always
on a log scale

00:05:04.740 --> 00:05:07.290
so you can stretch out and see
all the different technologies

00:05:07.290 --> 00:05:09.000
on one plot, right.

00:05:09.000 --> 00:05:12.180
And one of the nice things about
a plot is that this is energy,

00:05:12.180 --> 00:05:17.370
this is energy, and this
is energy per time, right.

00:05:17.370 --> 00:05:21.150
And so that's energy
per time times

00:05:21.150 --> 00:05:24.610
time, so we can have
lots of fun with that.

00:05:24.610 --> 00:05:28.050
But so what that means if you
think about it a little bit,

00:05:28.050 --> 00:05:30.900
if you plot energy storage
technologies on plots

00:05:30.900 --> 00:05:37.710
like this, then these diagonal
lines are one number of time.

00:05:37.710 --> 00:05:39.210
It's like if you
had a given density

00:05:39.210 --> 00:05:41.850
and you're using
it at a given rate,

00:05:41.850 --> 00:05:45.730
a giving power, then that's
how long it would last.

00:05:45.730 --> 00:05:47.700
So these plots are
really convenient.

00:05:47.700 --> 00:05:51.000
They're really convenient ways
to compare energy technologies.

00:05:51.000 --> 00:05:55.020
OK, so those would be like, I've
got energy storage technology

00:05:55.020 --> 00:05:57.930
and it's got this much density,
this much power-- well,

00:05:57.930 --> 00:06:01.770
then it's going to last if I
use it at that power for 41 days

00:06:01.770 --> 00:06:02.350
and so forth.

00:06:02.350 --> 00:06:05.470
Now the interesting, though,
there's lots of stuff on here.

00:06:05.470 --> 00:06:08.490
Why don't I just
want high and high?

00:06:08.490 --> 00:06:10.050
That sounds like a
good thing, right,

00:06:10.050 --> 00:06:15.330
so shouldn't we be pumping water
up hills all over the place?

00:06:15.330 --> 00:06:17.180
Well, obviously, if
you think about it,

00:06:17.180 --> 00:06:19.393
you don't do that in your phone.

00:06:19.393 --> 00:06:21.060
You don't even do
that in your backyard.

00:06:21.060 --> 00:06:22.930
You don't even do
that in a town.

00:06:22.930 --> 00:06:23.430
Why?

00:06:23.430 --> 00:06:27.340
Because pumped hydro is
extraordinarily limited.

00:06:27.340 --> 00:06:29.870
You have to do it at
very large scales.

00:06:29.870 --> 00:06:32.490
It's very low energy
density, right.

00:06:32.490 --> 00:06:35.310
It's geographically
highly constrained.

00:06:35.310 --> 00:06:40.440
It is one of the oldest ways we
have of storing energy, right.

00:06:40.440 --> 00:06:42.030
I'm just thinking
of the waterwheel,

00:06:42.030 --> 00:06:45.930
but it's very constrained,
so we can't use this in most

00:06:45.930 --> 00:06:48.630
applications, pumped hydro.

00:06:48.630 --> 00:06:51.300
But OK, oh, so this
is compressed air.

00:06:51.300 --> 00:06:53.262
Compressed Air, CAES.

00:06:53.262 --> 00:06:53.970
That sounds good.

00:06:53.970 --> 00:06:56.280
That's got a whole lot
of limitations as well.

00:06:56.280 --> 00:06:58.980
We've got, just to give
you the abbreviations,

00:06:58.980 --> 00:07:03.228
Superconducting Magnetic Coils.

00:07:03.228 --> 00:07:05.020
Superconducters, right,
just keep it going.

00:07:05.020 --> 00:07:06.450
And then you draw the
[MUMBLES] and it just

00:07:06.450 --> 00:07:07.330
keeps going forever.

00:07:07.330 --> 00:07:10.590
It's a great battery, right,
except for all the problems

00:07:10.590 --> 00:07:13.590
that it has and
how much it costs,

00:07:13.590 --> 00:07:16.090
but it's a really cool
concept and it's here.

00:07:16.090 --> 00:07:17.510
All of these things exist.

00:07:17.510 --> 00:07:19.020
All of these things exist.

00:07:19.020 --> 00:07:23.040
They all exist and they're all
very interesting and important

00:07:23.040 --> 00:07:24.990
energy storage devices.

00:07:24.990 --> 00:07:28.080
They're interesting, important,
electrical energy storage

00:07:28.080 --> 00:07:28.740
devices, right.

00:07:28.740 --> 00:07:31.110
So that's the main point.

00:07:31.110 --> 00:07:32.910
So like here, you
know supercaps,

00:07:32.910 --> 00:07:35.340
you build up a lot
of charge in a solid.

00:07:35.340 --> 00:07:36.750
Here you've got flywheels.

00:07:36.750 --> 00:07:39.010
Well, I'm just rotating
something heavy really,

00:07:39.010 --> 00:07:40.110
really fast.

00:07:40.110 --> 00:07:41.730
I get worried about that.

00:07:41.730 --> 00:07:43.500
I don't want to be
in that building,

00:07:43.500 --> 00:07:46.800
but they do build flywheel
energy storage device where

00:07:46.800 --> 00:07:52.120
you slow it down and then you
generate electricity from that.

00:07:52.120 --> 00:07:54.470
So these kinds of plots
are really, really,

00:07:54.470 --> 00:08:00.480
really helpful for comparing one
energy technology with another.

00:08:00.480 --> 00:08:04.810
Now, the thing is as I said,
there's a whole list of these

00:08:04.810 --> 00:08:06.670
and it goes on and on.

00:08:06.670 --> 00:08:07.930
Oh, that's really hard to see.

00:08:07.930 --> 00:08:10.360
They've all got a lot of
limitations, all right,

00:08:10.360 --> 00:08:14.680
and so capacitors
have a low density.

00:08:14.680 --> 00:08:17.350
You can convert
energy into some fuel

00:08:17.350 --> 00:08:18.790
and store it and
then combust it.

00:08:18.790 --> 00:08:20.977
That's got low
round-trip efficiency.

00:08:20.977 --> 00:08:23.560
This is what we're going to talk
about today, electrochemical.

00:08:23.560 --> 00:08:25.480
There are challenges there too.

00:08:25.480 --> 00:08:28.090
Kinetic, pumped hydro,
there's so many different ways

00:08:28.090 --> 00:08:31.000
of storing energy.

00:08:31.000 --> 00:08:33.820
The thing is there
is an advantage

00:08:33.820 --> 00:08:38.120
that I want to mention about
using electrochemistry.

00:08:38.120 --> 00:08:42.320
There is an advantage about
using electrochemistry,

00:08:42.320 --> 00:08:43.299
and what is electro--

00:08:43.299 --> 00:08:45.710
I just said the word
electrochemistry.

00:08:45.710 --> 00:08:47.900
I should tell you
what that is, right,

00:08:47.900 --> 00:09:03.990
and it's the relationship
between electricity

00:09:03.990 --> 00:09:08.150
and chemical reactions.

00:09:12.370 --> 00:09:15.990
I love saving time, rxns.

00:09:15.990 --> 00:09:19.590
That's equal to
electrochemistry.

00:09:19.590 --> 00:09:20.640
This isn't it.

00:09:20.640 --> 00:09:24.408
Now electrochemistry, OK.

00:09:24.408 --> 00:09:25.950
And so what you'll
see is like things

00:09:25.950 --> 00:09:29.220
like electrochemical
energy storage,

00:09:29.220 --> 00:09:34.090
and this isn't just
one type of thing.

00:09:34.090 --> 00:09:37.090
It's not just one
type of battery.

00:09:37.090 --> 00:09:38.980
It's not even just batteries.

00:09:38.980 --> 00:09:43.420
It's this broader
definition, but we're going

00:09:43.420 --> 00:09:44.990
to talk about batteries today.

00:09:44.990 --> 00:09:48.740
Now, so there's something
else that's really useful.

00:09:48.740 --> 00:09:51.280
So I mentioned we're
getting to the point

00:09:51.280 --> 00:09:53.980
where we're
electrifying everything.

00:09:53.980 --> 00:09:56.690
We need electricity
almost everywhere,

00:09:56.690 --> 00:09:58.960
even in your combustion car.

00:09:58.960 --> 00:10:01.300
You need electricity a
lot more than you used to,

00:10:01.300 --> 00:10:03.790
than 20 years ago.

00:10:03.790 --> 00:10:07.660
And so as we
electrify our world,

00:10:07.660 --> 00:10:10.230
and then I said I've got
to store that energy,

00:10:10.230 --> 00:10:13.360
you start to think
about what Carnot did.

00:10:13.360 --> 00:10:16.360
Now I'm paraphrasing here,
but this is basically

00:10:16.360 --> 00:10:18.730
what he said if you think
about how it applies

00:10:18.730 --> 00:10:22.240
to this conversion, which
is that the second law

00:10:22.240 --> 00:10:27.070
of thermodynamics makes
you pay a penalty of energy

00:10:27.070 --> 00:10:31.150
if you convert it into heat
and then back into electricity.

00:10:31.150 --> 00:10:34.000
Well, even if you just have
heat in the first place

00:10:34.000 --> 00:10:36.720
and now you want to generate
electricity from that,

00:10:36.720 --> 00:10:38.882
you're limited.

00:10:38.882 --> 00:10:40.340
We talked about
this before, right,

00:10:40.340 --> 00:10:47.060
like that the energy density
of gasoline, it's so high

00:10:47.060 --> 00:10:50.330
and it's such an amazing energy
storage material because you

00:10:50.330 --> 00:10:53.870
can transport it safely, right.

00:10:53.870 --> 00:10:57.210
Yeah, but the thing is, I just
gave you the example the car.

00:10:57.210 --> 00:10:58.450
You put it in your car.

00:10:58.450 --> 00:11:01.190
Does anybody know how efficient,
how much of that energy

00:11:01.190 --> 00:11:07.200
your car actually uses
to move percentage wise?

00:11:07.200 --> 00:11:09.190
Take a guess?

00:11:09.190 --> 00:11:11.310
Yeah, it's 10% to 20%,
maybe, 10% to 20%.

00:11:11.310 --> 00:11:14.600
It depends on the car--
my car probably 5%.

00:11:14.600 --> 00:11:18.250
It's an old car.

00:11:18.250 --> 00:11:21.510
So most of that gasoline's
energy is going into waste.

00:11:21.510 --> 00:11:22.290
Why?

00:11:22.290 --> 00:11:23.730
Carnot.

00:11:23.730 --> 00:11:25.410
Now this is not a
thermodynamics class,

00:11:25.410 --> 00:11:27.210
but this comes into
this so I wanted

00:11:27.210 --> 00:11:31.080
to mention Carnot and the
fact that there is another way

00:11:31.080 --> 00:11:32.390
to do it.

00:11:32.390 --> 00:11:34.680
So you could do chemical
reaction to heat.

00:11:34.680 --> 00:11:37.650
That's how we power
most of our world today.

00:11:37.650 --> 00:11:40.780
A power plant gets up
to a higher temperature

00:11:40.780 --> 00:11:43.320
so the penalty is less, but
still most power plants,

00:11:43.320 --> 00:11:45.970
you lose about 50%.

00:11:45.970 --> 00:11:48.700
Most power plants at
best are around 50%

00:11:48.700 --> 00:11:52.280
efficient in terms of
converting the thermal energy

00:11:52.280 --> 00:11:53.900
into electrical energy.

00:11:53.900 --> 00:11:56.810
Yeah, but I can use
a different way,

00:11:56.810 --> 00:11:58.130
and that's electrochemistry.

00:11:58.130 --> 00:12:00.380
And it's one of the reasons
why electrochemical energy

00:12:00.380 --> 00:12:06.130
storage is so appealing, because
you don't really pay a penalty.

00:12:06.130 --> 00:12:07.480
You don't really pay a penalty.

00:12:07.480 --> 00:12:12.360
I can go back and forth without
the second law hurting me.

00:12:12.360 --> 00:12:17.030
So I want to mention
something about Carnot,

00:12:17.030 --> 00:12:21.320
about the second law, and again,
this is not a class on thermo,

00:12:21.320 --> 00:12:23.330
but the second law
involves entropy

00:12:23.330 --> 00:12:26.990
so I had to say something,
especially because it's

00:12:26.990 --> 00:12:29.960
in Averill, right, and that's
your textbook that you're

00:12:29.960 --> 00:12:34.362
diligently reading every
day, and there it is.

00:12:34.362 --> 00:12:36.320
And by the way, Averill
has a beautiful chapter

00:12:36.320 --> 00:12:37.860
on batteries, chapter 19.

00:12:37.860 --> 00:12:39.350
I'm sure you know.

00:12:39.350 --> 00:12:40.858
It's really well written.

00:12:40.858 --> 00:12:42.650
This is what he says
about entropy, though,

00:12:42.650 --> 00:12:46.010
and for now we can
state that entropy

00:12:46.010 --> 00:12:48.320
is a thermodynamic property
of all substances that

00:12:48.320 --> 00:12:51.750
is proportional to their
degree of disorder.

00:12:51.750 --> 00:12:56.945
Averill, such a good book, but
let me ask you guys a question.

00:13:01.270 --> 00:13:03.920
So people think about
entropy as disorder

00:13:03.920 --> 00:13:08.510
and they think about entropy
as sort of more disorder.

00:13:08.510 --> 00:13:10.820
Let me ask you,
which one of these

00:13:10.820 --> 00:13:14.180
has a higher entropy,
the one on the left

00:13:14.180 --> 00:13:15.180
or the one on the right?

00:13:19.685 --> 00:13:21.920
So how many of you think
the one on the left

00:13:21.920 --> 00:13:23.960
has a higher entropy?

00:13:23.960 --> 00:13:27.930
How many of you think
it's the one on the right?

00:13:27.930 --> 00:13:31.730
So the thing is it's
the one on the left.

00:13:31.730 --> 00:13:33.140
Yeah, right.

00:13:33.140 --> 00:13:36.620
I love that you
guys are blown away.

00:13:36.620 --> 00:13:37.340
Why?

00:13:37.340 --> 00:13:40.430
Because entropy is not
about smoothness and it's

00:13:40.430 --> 00:13:41.930
not about disorder.

00:13:41.930 --> 00:13:44.540
That is wrong.

00:13:44.540 --> 00:13:48.370
Entropy is about
accessibility to states.

00:13:48.370 --> 00:13:53.080
It's about how many
states you have to be in.

00:13:53.080 --> 00:13:56.950
Now, that can appear to
be like disorder, right,

00:13:56.950 --> 00:14:00.580
but a messier room does
not have higher entropy,

00:14:00.580 --> 00:14:02.500
and in this case,
it's just simply

00:14:02.500 --> 00:14:04.090
the rules of the algorithm.

00:14:04.090 --> 00:14:07.780
I have rules here about the dots
being able to touch each other.

00:14:07.780 --> 00:14:10.300
They are randomly selected but
with the constraint that they

00:14:10.300 --> 00:14:12.680
cannot touch each other.

00:14:12.680 --> 00:14:16.060
That means that those dots
had fewer possibilities.

00:14:16.060 --> 00:14:18.490
They had fewer states
that they could be in.

00:14:18.490 --> 00:14:21.017
This system has
much higher entropy.

00:14:21.017 --> 00:14:22.350
It's about the number of states.

00:14:22.350 --> 00:14:23.730
I just want to make that--

00:14:23.730 --> 00:14:25.680
again, this is not the
topic of this class,

00:14:25.680 --> 00:14:28.920
but I've got to make my case
about entropy when I can.

00:14:28.920 --> 00:14:31.860
And I brought up
Carnot, so there you go.

00:14:31.860 --> 00:14:33.750
Entropy is not just disorder.

00:14:33.750 --> 00:14:35.370
Now OK, so we're
back to batteries.

00:14:35.370 --> 00:14:38.010
Now so electrochemistry,
there's a lot

00:14:38.010 --> 00:14:42.490
of reasons why
electrochemical storage is--

00:14:42.490 --> 00:14:43.740
here's the chart again, right.

00:14:43.740 --> 00:14:44.880
It's this one here.

00:14:44.880 --> 00:14:47.128
You see that, electrochemical
batteries, all right,

00:14:47.128 --> 00:14:48.420
that we're going to talk about.

00:14:48.420 --> 00:14:51.780
There's a lot of reasons
why this is so appealing.

00:14:51.780 --> 00:14:54.970
No penalty on the second
law is one of them.

00:14:54.970 --> 00:14:57.930
You can have transport
easily, right.

00:14:57.930 --> 00:14:59.000
We drive electric cars.

00:14:59.000 --> 00:14:59.550
We see that.

00:14:59.550 --> 00:15:01.650
No pollution at the
point of use-- pollution

00:15:01.650 --> 00:15:05.993
can be focused at the
point of production.

00:15:05.993 --> 00:15:07.410
Also, the batteries
can be charged

00:15:07.410 --> 00:15:11.543
by renewable technologies
like solar wind,

00:15:11.543 --> 00:15:12.960
and they're very
highly efficient.

00:15:12.960 --> 00:15:17.070
Electric cars, for example,
have a very high efficiency

00:15:17.070 --> 00:15:19.610
depending on the car,
but upwards of 90%

00:15:19.610 --> 00:15:22.110
in terms of converting
that electrical energy back

00:15:22.110 --> 00:15:23.100
and forth.

00:15:23.100 --> 00:15:25.850
The metrics that matter, I
wrote a couple of them here,

00:15:25.850 --> 00:15:27.930
all right, watts, hours.

00:15:27.930 --> 00:15:30.750
These are the things
that matter in batteries,

00:15:30.750 --> 00:15:32.800
but so many other things
matter, and this is why

00:15:32.800 --> 00:15:34.050
this is a complicated problem.

00:15:34.050 --> 00:15:38.970
And it's also why batteries
are not in any way

00:15:38.970 --> 00:15:40.360
a one-size-fits-all.

00:15:40.360 --> 00:15:44.820
I mean, you know that from
like AAs and AAAs and 9-volt,

00:15:44.820 --> 00:15:46.980
but it's so much more
than that, because it's

00:15:46.980 --> 00:15:51.000
about the application change
not going from your computer

00:15:51.000 --> 00:15:58.170
to your phone but going
from your car to your phone

00:15:58.170 --> 00:16:00.420
to a house to a grid.

00:16:00.420 --> 00:16:03.870
And so the needs are all
going to be different,

00:16:03.870 --> 00:16:06.540
and then which ones of
these things you care about

00:16:06.540 --> 00:16:09.203
is going to depend on what
chemistry you think about.

00:16:09.203 --> 00:16:10.620
It all comes down
to the chemistry

00:16:10.620 --> 00:16:11.670
as we're going to see.

00:16:11.670 --> 00:16:14.590
It's all about the chemistry.

00:16:14.590 --> 00:16:16.090
And just to put a
few numbers down,

00:16:16.090 --> 00:16:18.430
the cell phone has a
few watts of power.

00:16:18.430 --> 00:16:20.250
Light bulb is 10 to 100.

00:16:20.250 --> 00:16:24.800
A AAA battery has 1.5
watt hours of storage,

00:16:24.800 --> 00:16:28.030
all right, so that's how
much energy is inside

00:16:28.030 --> 00:16:29.710
of a AAA battery.

00:16:29.710 --> 00:16:32.560
Needed to drive a car 200
miles, 100 kilowatt hours.

00:16:32.560 --> 00:16:34.710
It's just fun to think
about these things, right.

00:16:34.710 --> 00:16:36.085
Powering your
house, 40 kilowatts

00:16:36.085 --> 00:16:39.880
max, depending on how many
AC units you're running.

00:16:39.880 --> 00:16:44.750
The world is this many
watts as of a few years ago.

00:16:44.750 --> 00:16:47.560
So if you had, I
think it's something

00:16:47.560 --> 00:16:50.140
like 20,000 billion
AAA batteries,

00:16:50.140 --> 00:16:53.393
you could run the world for an
hour-ish, something like that.

00:16:53.393 --> 00:16:55.060
These numbers are
just fun to play with,

00:16:55.060 --> 00:16:57.020
so I wanted to put
a few examples down.

00:16:57.020 --> 00:16:58.510
So where'd it all begin?

00:16:58.510 --> 00:17:02.450
It all began with an argument.

00:17:02.450 --> 00:17:06.260
It all began with this
beautiful argument

00:17:06.260 --> 00:17:08.510
that I have to tell
you because it's really

00:17:08.510 --> 00:17:10.400
a cool piece of history.

00:17:10.400 --> 00:17:12.589
And it took place in Italy.

00:17:12.589 --> 00:17:16.849
And it was Galvani who
was a physiologist,

00:17:16.849 --> 00:17:22.140
and he really loved the topic
of why animals move, right.

00:17:22.140 --> 00:17:24.440
So what is motion?

00:17:24.440 --> 00:17:27.920
How is it that
animals are moving?

00:17:27.920 --> 00:17:29.660
You know, how am I
moving my muscles?

00:17:29.660 --> 00:17:31.070
How is anything moving?

00:17:31.070 --> 00:17:33.710
Well, there had been
theories about this

00:17:33.710 --> 00:17:35.060
going all the way back.

00:17:35.060 --> 00:17:39.980
You remember Socrates and
Plato, the elements, right,

00:17:39.980 --> 00:17:41.240
and aether?

00:17:41.240 --> 00:17:45.720
There were similar theories
for motion of the human body.

00:17:45.720 --> 00:17:50.000
In fact, the word
pneuma was used

00:17:50.000 --> 00:17:53.900
to describe the essence that
goes through all living things

00:17:53.900 --> 00:17:55.480
that allows them to move.

00:17:55.480 --> 00:17:57.620
Well, Galvani didn't
like that and he

00:17:57.620 --> 00:18:00.320
wanted something more
concrete, and so he was really

00:18:00.320 --> 00:18:02.000
trying to make a
connection, especially

00:18:02.000 --> 00:18:05.213
between things that
move and electricity.

00:18:08.110 --> 00:18:10.930
Now, it was difficult. There
wasn't like a constant source.

00:18:10.930 --> 00:18:14.340
So you had electric generators
in your lab at the time,

00:18:14.340 --> 00:18:18.180
but Galvani wanted to go big, so
he hooked up his lightning rod

00:18:18.180 --> 00:18:19.750
to a frog.

00:18:19.750 --> 00:18:22.530
The frog was not alive.

00:18:22.530 --> 00:18:24.690
Well, the frog was
not alive, seriously,

00:18:24.690 --> 00:18:26.790
because then you'll see why--

00:18:26.790 --> 00:18:29.140
at least I don't think so.

00:18:29.140 --> 00:18:30.130
I really hope not.

00:18:30.130 --> 00:18:32.380
Now I'm thinking about it,
I'm getting a little bummed

00:18:32.380 --> 00:18:35.570
honestly, but I don't
think they were alive.

00:18:35.570 --> 00:18:37.080
That doesn't look alive.

00:18:37.080 --> 00:18:39.810
So he hooked them up to
lightning rods and he watched

00:18:39.810 --> 00:18:42.660
and he waited as the storm's
coming, and he's like,

00:18:42.660 --> 00:18:45.760
the lightning rod hits and he
hooked him up to some metals,

00:18:45.760 --> 00:18:48.110
and the frog went crazy.

00:18:48.110 --> 00:18:51.150
It was like, ah-ha,
motion, electricity.

00:18:51.150 --> 00:18:52.710
He'd been jolting
them in his lab

00:18:52.710 --> 00:18:56.400
with his own electric generator,
but he wanted to go big,

00:18:56.400 --> 00:18:58.650
and also, they didn't know.

00:18:58.650 --> 00:19:04.207
They didn't know at the time
what electricity was, right,

00:19:04.207 --> 00:19:06.540
so they didn't know if the
electricity that he generated

00:19:06.540 --> 00:19:08.850
in his own thing was the
same as the electricity that

00:19:08.850 --> 00:19:09.600
came from the sky.

00:19:09.600 --> 00:19:14.870
He saw that you get the same
result. The frog still moved.

00:19:14.870 --> 00:19:19.770
So Galvani said the
motion of a frog,

00:19:19.770 --> 00:19:22.980
and all motion of
all living creatures,

00:19:22.980 --> 00:19:26.070
generates electricity.

00:19:26.070 --> 00:19:26.940
That's what he said.

00:19:26.940 --> 00:19:29.680
He deduced that
the motion itself

00:19:29.680 --> 00:19:32.330
is something that
generates electricity.

00:19:32.330 --> 00:19:35.220
This is a really cool little
article about this debate,

00:19:35.220 --> 00:19:38.880
because in Galvani's case,
if you have two metal wires

00:19:38.880 --> 00:19:41.670
hooked up to a frog, like we
say, if you close the loop,

00:19:41.670 --> 00:19:44.190
then what he said is that
the charge that was already

00:19:44.190 --> 00:19:47.160
in there moves around
the loop and back

00:19:47.160 --> 00:19:49.490
and makes the leg move.

00:19:49.490 --> 00:19:52.620
Volta got really interested
in this and he said, you know,

00:19:52.620 --> 00:19:53.490
let me study this.

00:19:53.490 --> 00:19:58.350
He was a physicist at
the time and he said,

00:19:58.350 --> 00:20:00.300
this looks fascinating,
let me study it--

00:20:00.300 --> 00:20:02.850
but he said, you
know, why do you keep

00:20:02.850 --> 00:20:03.975
using two different metals?

00:20:07.280 --> 00:20:11.653
So I think this is about
the metals, not the frog,

00:20:11.653 --> 00:20:13.320
and they had a big
huge argument, right.

00:20:13.320 --> 00:20:15.840
So this is Volta's
picture, is that it's

00:20:15.840 --> 00:20:18.060
when you connect two
different metals that you

00:20:18.060 --> 00:20:22.090
get a charge flowing
through the frog.

00:20:22.090 --> 00:20:24.262
Volta, I think, as
many of you know,

00:20:24.262 --> 00:20:28.370
went on to win that
argument, and we

00:20:28.370 --> 00:20:31.070
have the volt named
after him, and he also

00:20:31.070 --> 00:20:36.490
created the first stable
battery, the voltaic pile.

00:20:36.490 --> 00:20:40.730
Galvani on the other hand really
opened the doors to the idea

00:20:40.730 --> 00:20:44.180
that we have electricity in our
bodies, which is pretty cool.

00:20:44.180 --> 00:20:46.730
He also is the reason why
the story of Frankenstein

00:20:46.730 --> 00:20:48.350
was written.

00:20:48.350 --> 00:20:54.290
It's actually true because
they went around and took

00:20:54.290 --> 00:20:57.890
all sorts of things and
electrocuted them and showed

00:20:57.890 --> 00:21:00.800
that they moved, and
often they weren't alive,

00:21:00.800 --> 00:21:03.170
and so Mary Shelley,
I think, saw that.

00:21:03.170 --> 00:21:06.110
You went around like the
countryside shocking people,

00:21:06.110 --> 00:21:09.770
and I actually meant that
as like not literally,

00:21:09.770 --> 00:21:14.553
but just shocking them
by what he's doing.

00:21:14.553 --> 00:21:16.220
And I don't think
they ever used humans,

00:21:16.220 --> 00:21:19.230
but still, people's
imaginations and so forth,

00:21:19.230 --> 00:21:21.060
and it was quite a thing.

00:21:21.060 --> 00:21:23.477
OK, very interesting story.

00:21:23.477 --> 00:21:24.560
Now, why does this matter?

00:21:24.560 --> 00:21:25.810
I've got two why this matters.

00:21:25.810 --> 00:21:29.210
Today my first one is on a
personal scale, smaller scale,

00:21:29.210 --> 00:21:32.570
and then my next one
is on a grid scale.

00:21:32.570 --> 00:21:35.005
So this is called a little sun.

00:21:35.005 --> 00:21:36.380
There's over a
billion people who

00:21:36.380 --> 00:21:39.050
can't read at night
because there's

00:21:39.050 --> 00:21:42.225
no access to electricity,
and of course,

00:21:42.225 --> 00:21:43.850
a lot of the studying
that you could do

00:21:43.850 --> 00:21:45.110
would be at night, right.

00:21:45.110 --> 00:21:47.000
So this is a really
cool program.

00:21:47.000 --> 00:21:47.900
Why does this work?

00:21:47.900 --> 00:21:49.483
This is a solar cell
but this wouldn't

00:21:49.483 --> 00:21:51.890
work unless you could store
the electricity, right,

00:21:51.890 --> 00:21:54.340
so I wanted to make you
guys aware of Little Sun.

00:21:54.340 --> 00:21:57.290
You just press this and you
can read at night, right.

00:21:57.290 --> 00:21:59.330
This is already making
a big difference

00:21:59.330 --> 00:22:03.520
in millions of people's lives.

00:22:03.520 --> 00:22:05.050
That doesn't happen
unless you've

00:22:05.050 --> 00:22:07.890
got electrochemical
energy storage,

00:22:07.890 --> 00:22:12.960
right, and there's
some stats up here.

00:22:12.960 --> 00:22:15.160
$25 billion liters
of kerosene are

00:22:15.160 --> 00:22:19.060
used to meet the basic lighting
needs in a lot of these places.

00:22:19.060 --> 00:22:23.950
That releases tremendous
amounts of toxic fumes,

00:22:23.950 --> 00:22:27.740
and you can make
a huge difference,

00:22:27.740 --> 00:22:31.240
But you know, it's sort of
the same as cooking, right.

00:22:31.240 --> 00:22:35.080
So wood fire cooking
is a huge problem.

00:22:35.080 --> 00:22:37.630
So the challenge is,
but you don't cook.

00:22:37.630 --> 00:22:40.000
If you have a solar
cooker, that's

00:22:40.000 --> 00:22:42.970
great except that most people
don't cook during the day.

00:22:42.970 --> 00:22:45.710
They cook at night or
in the morning, right.

00:22:45.710 --> 00:22:48.610
And so you've got
to store energy

00:22:48.610 --> 00:22:51.490
to make these things
actually useful, right,

00:22:51.490 --> 00:22:53.410
and I really like
this program so I

00:22:53.410 --> 00:22:55.600
wanted to make that one
of my, why this matters.

00:22:55.600 --> 00:22:58.060
And we'll go big
on the next one,

00:22:58.060 --> 00:22:59.690
but let's get to the chemistry.

00:22:59.690 --> 00:23:02.230
So what happens in a battery?

00:23:02.230 --> 00:23:05.020
The best way to understand
this is with a classic example

00:23:05.020 --> 00:23:06.070
of two different metals.

00:23:06.070 --> 00:23:08.310
It's Volta's two
different metals.

00:23:08.310 --> 00:23:10.040
Volta did lots of metals.

00:23:10.040 --> 00:23:11.560
That's what he did.

00:23:11.560 --> 00:23:16.540
And he showed that the frog
moved with most of them,

00:23:16.540 --> 00:23:17.410
so it's the metals.

00:23:17.410 --> 00:23:22.100
So let's take these two classic
metals and show what happens.

00:23:22.100 --> 00:23:25.300
You've got copper
and you've got zinc.

00:23:25.300 --> 00:23:27.400
Now, in this case, I'm
just going to have a zinc.

00:23:27.400 --> 00:23:30.640
This is the zinc piece of metal,
and this is a piece of zinc,

00:23:30.640 --> 00:23:35.230
and I put it in a
solution of copper ions.

00:23:35.230 --> 00:23:38.270
And so in this case,
what do we have?

00:23:38.270 --> 00:23:39.730
Let's write this down.

00:23:39.730 --> 00:23:48.490
In this case, I've
got zinc solid.

00:23:51.040 --> 00:23:53.680
Well, before I write this
down, let's write down

00:23:53.680 --> 00:23:55.330
what's in the solution.

00:23:55.330 --> 00:23:58.990
The solution is copper sulfate
and this goes to everything

00:23:58.990 --> 00:24:00.860
that we've been talking about.

00:24:00.860 --> 00:24:03.850
So I've got a
solution, a beaker,

00:24:03.850 --> 00:24:06.940
that has copper ions in it.

00:24:06.940 --> 00:24:07.823
How much?

00:24:07.823 --> 00:24:08.365
I don't know.

00:24:08.365 --> 00:24:10.780
Look up the K sp, right?

00:24:10.780 --> 00:24:12.910
Now we know how to do that.

00:24:12.910 --> 00:24:16.510
So 2 minus in solution.

00:24:16.510 --> 00:24:20.200
So you put some of this copper
sulfate powder in that beaker.

00:24:20.200 --> 00:24:22.630
It turns blue and it
gives you these ions that

00:24:22.630 --> 00:24:26.120
are dissolved in solution, and
then you put a piece of zinc

00:24:26.120 --> 00:24:26.620
in there.

00:24:30.040 --> 00:24:37.470
So what happens is there is a
transfer of electrons, right.

00:24:37.470 --> 00:24:39.438
Now we talked a lot
about electrons.

00:24:39.438 --> 00:24:41.230
We're talking about
transfers of electrons.

00:24:41.230 --> 00:24:42.688
I mean, just think
about it, right.

00:24:42.688 --> 00:24:46.170
We went from H,
right, like this.

00:24:46.170 --> 00:24:50.820
Look at that, right, the bonding
and then we did this, OK.

00:24:50.820 --> 00:24:51.360
Here we go.

00:24:51.360 --> 00:24:55.020
It's a little bit less
sharing but still.

00:24:55.020 --> 00:25:01.020
All right, and then we did
this, where it's like, OK,

00:25:01.020 --> 00:25:02.530
there's almost nothing there.

00:25:02.530 --> 00:25:03.280
It all went there.

00:25:03.280 --> 00:25:06.850
These are where my
electrons are, all right.

00:25:06.850 --> 00:25:13.220
Well, then we put this in
water and we got this, OK.

00:25:13.220 --> 00:25:14.930
And we got them
sort of separated

00:25:14.930 --> 00:25:18.440
and this one just broke
off with its electron,

00:25:18.440 --> 00:25:20.290
leaving a positive
charge behind.

00:25:20.290 --> 00:25:21.530
So we've done all this.

00:25:21.530 --> 00:25:24.690
We know this, all right.

00:25:24.690 --> 00:25:26.470
So what's happening
here is special

00:25:26.470 --> 00:25:30.310
because what's happening here
is a trading of electrons.

00:25:30.310 --> 00:25:32.860
You see, in all the
bonding that we've done,

00:25:32.860 --> 00:25:35.500
we're talking about how
electrons share in bonds

00:25:35.500 --> 00:25:38.040
and then these
things break apart,

00:25:38.040 --> 00:25:41.160
but now I'm going to do
reactions that trade them.

00:25:41.160 --> 00:25:42.990
That's electrochemistry.

00:25:42.990 --> 00:25:44.490
That's electrochemistry,
and what

00:25:44.490 --> 00:25:47.520
happens when I put that zinc
strip into that solution

00:25:47.520 --> 00:25:48.420
is this.

00:25:48.420 --> 00:25:55.690
The zinc that goes into solution
is 2 plus and 2 electrons.

00:25:55.690 --> 00:25:59.730
And the copper in
the solution plates

00:25:59.730 --> 00:26:03.630
onto the zinc, plus
those 2 electrons

00:26:03.630 --> 00:26:07.110
goes to copper like that.

00:26:07.110 --> 00:26:10.560
And if you look at
the full reaction,

00:26:10.560 --> 00:26:16.200
the full reaction would be
then zinc solid plus copper

00:26:16.200 --> 00:26:25.760
2 plus in solution goes to zinc
2 plus in solution plus copper

00:26:25.760 --> 00:26:26.840
solid.

00:26:26.840 --> 00:26:27.890
That's the trading.

00:26:27.890 --> 00:26:31.250
Now I like writing it like
this because you really

00:26:31.250 --> 00:26:34.730
see explicitly the gaining
and losing of electrons.

00:26:34.730 --> 00:26:37.280
Oh, those have words
associated with them,

00:26:37.280 --> 00:26:39.230
gaining and losing electrons.

00:26:39.230 --> 00:26:40.940
But this is the
reaction that we're

00:26:40.940 --> 00:26:44.720
kind of used to, right, so
this is just the same thing.

00:26:44.720 --> 00:26:47.000
It's what happens, right.

00:26:47.000 --> 00:26:47.880
It's what happens.

00:26:47.880 --> 00:26:50.820
It's a trade of electrons.

00:26:50.820 --> 00:26:54.200
Now it turns out that
when something loses

00:26:54.200 --> 00:26:58.680
electrons like that
we call it oxidation,

00:26:58.680 --> 00:27:01.740
and when something
gains electrons

00:27:01.740 --> 00:27:03.225
we call this reduction.

00:27:05.970 --> 00:27:10.810
And so when two
different materials--

00:27:10.810 --> 00:27:14.040
in this case, these two metals--
trade electrons and one of them

00:27:14.040 --> 00:27:16.360
is oxidized and one
of them is reduced,

00:27:16.360 --> 00:27:17.690
that's also got a name.

00:27:17.690 --> 00:27:19.450
It's called a redox couple.

00:27:19.450 --> 00:27:23.380
Those are a redox couple,
redox, redox, right.

00:27:26.680 --> 00:27:34.380
So these are a redox couple,
is what they're called when

00:27:34.380 --> 00:27:36.210
you trade electrons like this.

00:27:36.210 --> 00:27:38.620
OK, so what's happening?

00:27:38.620 --> 00:27:44.690
What's happening is at
the surface of the zinc,

00:27:44.690 --> 00:27:47.630
I see these copper
ions in solution.

00:27:47.630 --> 00:27:52.760
And it's like, well, if
I gave you two electrons,

00:27:52.760 --> 00:27:58.610
then you'd be able to become
a copper solid, right.

00:27:58.610 --> 00:28:02.540
So if I give you two electrons,
you're a copper solid,

00:28:02.540 --> 00:28:05.660
but now if I lose two
electrons as a zinc atom,

00:28:05.660 --> 00:28:07.550
I'm a zinc 2 plus.

00:28:07.550 --> 00:28:09.020
That's this.

00:28:09.020 --> 00:28:11.280
That's just this, right.

00:28:11.280 --> 00:28:12.880
Why does that happen?

00:28:12.880 --> 00:28:15.630
Well, we'll get to that, right,
but that's what's happening.

00:28:15.630 --> 00:28:18.690
So a zinc atom at
the surface, all

00:28:18.690 --> 00:28:21.490
right, a zinc atom at the
service sees a copper 2 plus

00:28:21.490 --> 00:28:22.997
and it says here,
have 2 electrons.

00:28:22.997 --> 00:28:24.580
Oh, and then it's
like, wait a second.

00:28:24.580 --> 00:28:25.663
I gave two electrons away.

00:28:25.663 --> 00:28:28.570
I've got to go into solution.

00:28:28.570 --> 00:28:31.060
And that's exactly what
you see over here, right.

00:28:31.060 --> 00:28:35.020
The copper is plating
the zinc and the zinc

00:28:35.020 --> 00:28:37.270
is coming off of the strip.

00:28:37.270 --> 00:28:38.320
They're doing this.

00:28:38.320 --> 00:28:40.970
All right, why is
that happening?

00:28:40.970 --> 00:28:43.420
Because if you put that
strip in there and the thing

00:28:43.420 --> 00:28:46.270
just heats up,
this is exothermic.

00:28:46.270 --> 00:28:48.250
That means that this
reaction will go.

00:28:48.250 --> 00:28:50.260
This is spontaneous.

00:28:50.260 --> 00:28:52.030
This is spontaneous.

00:28:52.030 --> 00:28:54.410
Where can I write that?

00:28:54.410 --> 00:28:56.300
Nowhere.

00:28:56.300 --> 00:28:57.170
Spontaneous.

00:29:03.900 --> 00:29:07.970
It didn't have to be, as
we'll see, but this one is.

00:29:07.970 --> 00:29:12.280
But all that's happening is
you're going down in energy.

00:29:12.280 --> 00:29:14.570
You're giving energy
away and you're

00:29:14.570 --> 00:29:15.720
heating up the environment.

00:29:15.720 --> 00:29:18.420
Now the question is, how
can I not let that happen?

00:29:18.420 --> 00:29:20.420
How can I stop the
heating from happen

00:29:20.420 --> 00:29:23.720
and instead take advantage
of this electron trading

00:29:23.720 --> 00:29:24.980
to do work?

00:29:24.980 --> 00:29:26.390
That's what a battery is.

00:29:26.390 --> 00:29:27.560
That's what a battery is.

00:29:27.560 --> 00:29:29.750
It's taking advantage of this.

00:29:29.750 --> 00:29:31.910
And really what it
comes down to is just

00:29:31.910 --> 00:29:34.250
a different construction
of the device.

00:29:34.250 --> 00:29:35.010
It's this.

00:29:35.010 --> 00:29:35.510
The

00:29:35.510 --> 00:29:38.690
Fundamentals of a battery
is this kind of thing,

00:29:38.690 --> 00:29:41.840
but now instead of allowing
this plating to happen,

00:29:41.840 --> 00:29:44.190
I'm going to
separate that and I'm

00:29:44.190 --> 00:29:46.890
going to construct
it in such a way

00:29:46.890 --> 00:29:51.180
that those electrons get
traded through some wire

00:29:51.180 --> 00:29:53.100
that I can do work with, OK.

00:29:53.100 --> 00:29:57.970
So for example-- where is it?

00:29:57.970 --> 00:30:00.160
OK, maybe not for example.

00:30:00.160 --> 00:30:06.840
Maybe for example, maybe I
need to turn this on and off.

00:30:06.840 --> 00:30:07.660
Here we go.

00:30:07.660 --> 00:30:10.050
This is the picture that
Averill gives for the battery.

00:30:10.050 --> 00:30:12.750
So what you saw wasn't a battery
because I wasn't stopping

00:30:12.750 --> 00:30:14.603
the electron flow
and doing anything,

00:30:14.603 --> 00:30:16.020
but if you construct
it like this,

00:30:16.020 --> 00:30:17.792
is called a galvanic cell.

00:30:17.792 --> 00:30:19.500
All right, if you
construct it like this,

00:30:19.500 --> 00:30:22.990
now I can do work with
that electron trade.

00:30:22.990 --> 00:30:24.750
I can do work with
the electron trade.

00:30:24.750 --> 00:30:25.720
That's the difference.

00:30:25.720 --> 00:30:26.890
So let's go through this.

00:30:26.890 --> 00:30:29.010
So on the left,
so what did I do?

00:30:29.010 --> 00:30:32.340
Well, I got my zinc strip but
now I've got a copper strip.

00:30:35.060 --> 00:30:36.940
Just trying to see.

00:30:36.940 --> 00:30:39.340
OK, I've got a copper
strip over here.

00:30:39.340 --> 00:30:41.740
I've got a zinc strip over here.

00:30:41.740 --> 00:30:44.050
But I'm putting the
zinc not into the copper

00:30:44.050 --> 00:30:47.110
directly where I just short
it and the whole thing

00:30:47.110 --> 00:30:47.830
just heats up.

00:30:47.830 --> 00:30:51.310
Instead, I'm putting the zinc
into a solution of zinc ions.

00:30:51.310 --> 00:30:52.450
Look at that.

00:30:52.450 --> 00:30:56.340
Zinc has zinc ions
in solution, right.

00:30:56.340 --> 00:31:00.530
Now, copper, copper is in
a solution of copper ions.

00:31:00.530 --> 00:31:02.200
So these are now
the two beakers.

00:31:02.200 --> 00:31:04.570
On the left I've
got zinc nitrate.

00:31:04.570 --> 00:31:07.300
In this case it's a
nitrate, not a sulfate.

00:31:07.300 --> 00:31:08.690
It doesn't matter.

00:31:08.690 --> 00:31:10.690
The whole point
of the solution is

00:31:10.690 --> 00:31:13.090
that I get these
ions in a beaker.

00:31:13.090 --> 00:31:15.010
Got to get these
ions in a beaker,

00:31:15.010 --> 00:31:19.455
so you've got the copper
ions in a beaker on the right

00:31:19.455 --> 00:31:21.580
with the copper solid and
you've got the zinc ions.

00:31:21.580 --> 00:31:22.955
So let's look at
what's happening

00:31:22.955 --> 00:31:25.390
and blow up those
different sides, right.

00:31:25.390 --> 00:31:27.820
So at the anode,
it's called the anode

00:31:27.820 --> 00:31:30.820
because it's where
the charge comes from.

00:31:30.820 --> 00:31:31.900
It's called the anode.

00:31:31.900 --> 00:31:32.650
What's happening?

00:31:32.650 --> 00:31:36.380
Well, what's happening is
what I just showed you.

00:31:36.380 --> 00:31:39.920
The zinc is leaving the solid.

00:31:39.920 --> 00:31:44.355
The zinc is leaving the solid
and it's becoming 2 plus.

00:31:44.355 --> 00:31:45.230
It's becoming 2 plus.

00:31:49.825 --> 00:31:52.200
But the thing is that now,
you've got to be careful here.

00:31:52.200 --> 00:31:55.510
So the zinc is becoming 2 plus.

00:31:55.510 --> 00:31:58.810
What about those two electrons?

00:31:58.810 --> 00:32:00.670
Where do they go?

00:32:00.670 --> 00:32:02.830
A zinc atom leaves
its metal strip

00:32:02.830 --> 00:32:07.840
and goes into that beaker
of zinc ions, right.

00:32:07.840 --> 00:32:11.011
But now you've got two charges,
two electrons on the strip

00:32:11.011 --> 00:32:13.690
because it left a zinc 2 plus.

00:32:13.690 --> 00:32:15.730
Nothing more will happen.

00:32:15.730 --> 00:32:19.910
Nothing more will happen unless
those 2 electrons go away.

00:32:19.910 --> 00:32:21.040
That's the whole point.

00:32:21.040 --> 00:32:23.680
What's so cool
about batteries is

00:32:23.680 --> 00:32:27.430
that it's all about neutrality.

00:32:27.430 --> 00:32:29.440
If you think about batteries
as current flowing,

00:32:29.440 --> 00:32:31.390
batteries is all
about neutrality.

00:32:31.390 --> 00:32:33.460
It's all about keeping
these things neutral

00:32:33.460 --> 00:32:36.520
because if I just build
this charge up here,

00:32:36.520 --> 00:32:38.800
I can't have another
zinc atom go in

00:32:38.800 --> 00:32:40.330
so the charge has
to go somewhere,

00:32:40.330 --> 00:32:42.490
but that's what
that wire is for.

00:32:42.490 --> 00:32:45.130
That's what this
wire is for, right.

00:32:45.130 --> 00:32:47.410
This wire allows the
charge to go over

00:32:47.410 --> 00:32:50.020
to the other place,
the cathode, where

00:32:50.020 --> 00:32:54.710
the copper is sitting there
saying, you know what,

00:32:54.710 --> 00:32:57.750
if I had two electrons--

00:32:57.750 --> 00:33:00.800
if I had two electrons
on the copper strip then

00:33:00.800 --> 00:33:03.665
I could use those as a way
to lure in a copper 2 plus

00:33:03.665 --> 00:33:05.737
ion, right.

00:33:05.737 --> 00:33:07.320
I could use those
as a way to lure it.

00:33:07.320 --> 00:33:12.580
And if I did that, then I'm
doing what this did right here.

00:33:12.580 --> 00:33:16.120
It's the same thing that we
just did with the plating

00:33:16.120 --> 00:33:19.570
except now those electrons
are going through this wire

00:33:19.570 --> 00:33:20.800
up here.

00:33:20.800 --> 00:33:23.290
The zinc is trading
electrons with the copper.

00:33:23.290 --> 00:33:26.980
That's keeping this neutral,
so now the electrons go away

00:33:26.980 --> 00:33:29.670
and now another zinc
can go into solution.

00:33:29.670 --> 00:33:32.700
Then the copper grabs them,
plates a piece of copper,

00:33:32.700 --> 00:33:35.400
and then more electrons
can come, right.

00:33:35.400 --> 00:33:36.860
It's the same thing.

00:33:36.860 --> 00:33:38.250
It's the same thing.

00:33:38.250 --> 00:33:39.510
This is what it looks like.

00:33:39.510 --> 00:33:42.570
If you take those electrodes
out of the solutions

00:33:42.570 --> 00:33:47.490
after running this for five, 10
minutes, look at what happened.

00:33:47.490 --> 00:33:49.220
The copper plated.

00:33:49.220 --> 00:33:50.820
You got copper
ions from solution

00:33:50.820 --> 00:33:55.937
plating that and zinc ions
from solution leaving that.

00:33:55.937 --> 00:33:58.020
And so those are the
reactions that are happening.

00:33:58.020 --> 00:33:59.400
One's happening on the
zinc side and one's

00:33:59.400 --> 00:34:01.733
happening on the other side,
but there's something else.

00:34:01.733 --> 00:34:04.590
There's something else
because there's just

00:34:04.590 --> 00:34:06.000
one other piece
of it that you've

00:34:06.000 --> 00:34:08.370
got to understand to
understand how a battery works,

00:34:08.370 --> 00:34:10.860
and it's this thing here.

00:34:10.860 --> 00:34:14.969
So up here, this is my
conduit for electrons to go.

00:34:14.969 --> 00:34:17.790
Each time it goes, I can plate.

00:34:17.790 --> 00:34:20.760
I can lose the more
it goes, right.

00:34:20.760 --> 00:34:25.639
But notice if I lose 2 plus--

00:34:25.639 --> 00:34:28.620
so I've got these 2 plus.

00:34:28.620 --> 00:34:31.300
It's not charged.

00:34:31.300 --> 00:34:32.610
Look at this.

00:34:32.610 --> 00:34:34.949
Copper sulfate in that
case is copper nitrate.

00:34:34.949 --> 00:34:37.500
Copper sulfate
goes in a solution,

00:34:37.500 --> 00:34:39.090
it's not a charged solution.

00:34:39.090 --> 00:34:43.449
These things are balanced,
right, 2 plus, 2 minus.

00:34:43.449 --> 00:34:46.020
So now I'm taking only a
2 plus out of solution.

00:34:46.020 --> 00:34:47.010
No way.

00:34:47.010 --> 00:34:49.210
It's the same thing.

00:34:49.210 --> 00:34:52.699
You've got make it
neutral again, all right.

00:34:52.699 --> 00:34:53.739
It won't work otherwise.

00:34:53.739 --> 00:34:56.060
You can't start building
up charge in the solution.

00:34:56.060 --> 00:34:57.910
So that's what this
thing is about.

00:34:57.910 --> 00:34:59.560
That's what this is.

00:34:59.560 --> 00:35:02.680
This is called a salt bridge
and that completes the circuit.

00:35:02.680 --> 00:35:04.660
See, this lets the
electrons flow.

00:35:04.660 --> 00:35:07.330
This gives ions.

00:35:07.330 --> 00:35:10.400
It doesn't matter
what it is, right.

00:35:10.400 --> 00:35:11.470
That's the electrolyte.

00:35:11.470 --> 00:35:13.110
It's just a source of ions.

00:35:13.110 --> 00:35:15.020
Here is an ACL.

00:35:15.020 --> 00:35:17.300
It's an ACL, so now
for every copper 2

00:35:17.300 --> 00:35:24.180
plus that plates onto the copper
electrode, two NA plus atoms go

00:35:24.180 --> 00:35:25.860
into this beaker
from the bridge,

00:35:25.860 --> 00:35:28.320
because here I've got
dissolved salt. Dissolved salt,

00:35:28.320 --> 00:35:30.990
sodium chloride is in water,
so I got all these sodium ions.

00:35:30.990 --> 00:35:34.200
I got all these chlorine ions.

00:35:34.200 --> 00:35:38.370
And when a charge imbalance
happens because something

00:35:38.370 --> 00:35:41.760
plates here, I can draw sodium
atoms out of the salt bridge

00:35:41.760 --> 00:35:44.193
and I can draw chlorine atoms
out of that side, right.

00:35:44.193 --> 00:35:45.360
That completes your circuit.

00:35:45.360 --> 00:35:48.090
That's an iron neutralizer,
the salt bridge,

00:35:48.090 --> 00:35:50.310
and this is allowing
the electrons

00:35:50.310 --> 00:35:52.110
to keep everything
neutral, and that's

00:35:52.110 --> 00:35:53.940
how you get the current, right.

00:35:53.940 --> 00:35:55.740
It's pretty cool.

00:35:55.740 --> 00:35:57.250
It's pretty cool stuff.

