00:39:15.890 --> 00:39:18.760
And you know, when
you look at this,

00:39:18.760 --> 00:39:21.990
you might say-- this is
from Averill so he actually

00:39:21.990 --> 00:39:24.690
is missing the f's because
this is just taken right

00:39:24.690 --> 00:39:31.778
from the textbook, but anyway,
so that's the potential.

00:39:31.778 --> 00:39:32.320
Look at this.

00:39:32.320 --> 00:39:34.840
This is like an energy diagram
but these aren't electronics.

00:39:34.840 --> 00:39:37.890
These are just potentials,
but the potential

00:39:37.890 --> 00:39:42.430
is related to which one
wants the 2 electrons more.

00:39:42.430 --> 00:39:43.720
It's 2 plus.

00:39:43.720 --> 00:39:48.070
The oxidation state is 2
plus, so you look at this,

00:39:48.070 --> 00:39:50.170
you say, well, I
don't know which one

00:39:50.170 --> 00:39:53.710
might give up electrons more.

00:39:53.710 --> 00:39:57.260
It turns out it's the zinc.

00:39:57.260 --> 00:40:00.175
And you might say,
well, but wait a second.

00:40:00.175 --> 00:40:01.800
If I just take an
electron out of here,

00:40:01.800 --> 00:40:04.320
won't it be harder
than one out of here,

00:40:04.320 --> 00:40:05.460
because this only has 4s1?

00:40:08.110 --> 00:40:11.110
The answer is yes,
but I'm taking 2.

00:40:11.110 --> 00:40:11.970
I'm not taking 1.

00:40:11.970 --> 00:40:16.930
I'm talking about the
oxidation state of 2 plus.

00:40:16.930 --> 00:40:18.760
That's going to be harder here.

00:40:18.760 --> 00:40:21.820
It's harder to
take a 4s1 in a 3d

00:40:21.820 --> 00:40:24.970
from that beautifully
filled 3d shell than it

00:40:24.970 --> 00:40:26.320
is to take these 2s electrons.

00:40:26.320 --> 00:40:27.550
That's why that's higher.

00:40:27.550 --> 00:40:30.220
That's why that's hire, right.

00:40:30.220 --> 00:40:37.540
So what you can see is that
the potential difference

00:40:37.540 --> 00:40:39.880
is all about chemistry, right.

00:40:39.880 --> 00:40:49.180
The delta v is all
about the chemistry.

00:40:49.180 --> 00:40:52.520
It's all about all of the
things that we've learned.

00:40:52.520 --> 00:40:55.460
It's more than that
because of these solutions,

00:40:55.460 --> 00:40:57.080
so it's more
complicated than that,

00:40:57.080 --> 00:41:00.940
but at the core of all of this
is all the topics that we've

00:41:00.940 --> 00:41:05.155
learned, how strongly are
electrons bound to an atom,

00:41:05.155 --> 00:41:07.870
to a metal, right.

00:41:07.870 --> 00:41:15.220
And so well, if I took
cobalt, what would happen?

00:41:15.220 --> 00:41:18.940
If I took cobalt
instead of copper,

00:41:18.940 --> 00:41:21.130
the voltage here would be 1/2.

00:41:21.130 --> 00:41:24.070
And you can sort of
look at the table

00:41:24.070 --> 00:41:25.240
and think about why that is.

00:41:25.240 --> 00:41:29.530
Cobalt has a 4s2
but it's got less

00:41:29.530 --> 00:41:33.320
d electrons, and so is
it going to be easier

00:41:33.320 --> 00:41:34.460
or harder to pull them out?

00:41:34.460 --> 00:41:36.920
Well, it's going to
be easier than copper

00:41:36.920 --> 00:41:40.670
but harder than zinc so it
goes in the middle, right.

00:41:40.670 --> 00:41:44.210
And so the potential difference
is less for cobalt and zinc

00:41:44.210 --> 00:41:46.490
but you still get a potential.

00:41:46.490 --> 00:41:48.680
And so that's what
Volta did, and he did

00:41:48.680 --> 00:41:51.260
all sorts of different metals.

00:41:51.260 --> 00:41:56.150
And you can understand why zinc
and copper does this but copper

00:41:56.150 --> 00:41:58.090
and zinc doesn't.

00:41:58.090 --> 00:41:59.590
That's copper in
the zinc solution.

00:41:59.590 --> 00:42:01.180
That's zinc in the
copper solution.

00:42:01.180 --> 00:42:03.340
Nothing happens.

00:42:03.340 --> 00:42:06.340
The reason is the potential.

00:42:06.340 --> 00:42:08.540
It's just this.

00:42:08.540 --> 00:42:11.660
It's just literally the
potential difference

00:42:11.660 --> 00:42:12.410
that we just drew.

00:42:12.410 --> 00:42:14.450
This is going to
be easier to take.

00:42:14.450 --> 00:42:17.113
So I'm not going to go
the other way, right.

00:42:17.113 --> 00:42:18.530
I'm not going to
go the other way,

00:42:18.530 --> 00:42:21.010
so nothing happens
when you do that.

00:42:21.010 --> 00:42:23.110
And then finally, the
last kind of piece of this

00:42:23.110 --> 00:42:26.350
is that we need a reference
because you can only

00:42:26.350 --> 00:42:28.348
get the changes in potentials.

00:42:28.348 --> 00:42:29.140
It's like enthalpy.

00:42:29.140 --> 00:42:31.330
You can only get the
changes, not the absolute,

00:42:31.330 --> 00:42:33.490
so you've got to have
a standardization.

00:42:33.490 --> 00:42:35.860
And the standardization
in the world of batteries

00:42:35.860 --> 00:42:40.800
is hydrogen. So
what you do when you

00:42:40.800 --> 00:42:44.550
think about battery
potentials is you write down

00:42:44.550 --> 00:42:48.765
all of these reduction--
reduction is gaining electrons,

00:42:48.765 --> 00:42:49.890
right-- you write down all.

00:42:49.890 --> 00:42:50.500
It doesn't matter.

00:42:50.500 --> 00:42:52.708
You could write reduction,
you could write oxidation.

00:42:52.708 --> 00:42:55.410
They're just a flip
of the sign, right.

00:42:55.410 --> 00:42:57.580
It's just a flipped
sign of the potential,

00:42:57.580 --> 00:42:59.940
but what you write
these down with respect

00:42:59.940 --> 00:43:02.310
to a common electrode,
and it's the standard.

00:43:02.310 --> 00:43:04.630
It's called the standard
hydrogen electrode.

00:43:04.630 --> 00:43:08.550
So sometimes you'll see it as
the SHE in the battery world.

00:43:08.550 --> 00:43:11.790
And what it is it's just a
very nice platinum electrode

00:43:11.790 --> 00:43:12.850
that doesn't change.

00:43:12.850 --> 00:43:15.900
So it doesn't plate
or lose atoms.

00:43:15.900 --> 00:43:18.120
And it's a reaction
that on that electrode

00:43:18.120 --> 00:43:21.550
happens with hydrogen,
hydrogen gas.

00:43:21.550 --> 00:43:24.678
And so what you do is you
hook this up on one side.

00:43:24.678 --> 00:43:27.220
You hook that up on one side,
and then you put all the metals

00:43:27.220 --> 00:43:28.190
against that.

00:43:28.190 --> 00:43:31.450
And you measure their potential.

00:43:31.450 --> 00:43:35.380
What's so powerful about
the SHE is that now I've

00:43:35.380 --> 00:43:38.140
got all of these with respect
to the same reference, right?

00:43:38.140 --> 00:43:40.845
So now when I look at
my zinc-copper battery,

00:43:40.845 --> 00:43:42.970
well, it's just the difference
between those that's

00:43:42.970 --> 00:43:45.540
the potential of the battery.

00:43:45.540 --> 00:43:48.620
So hydrogen electrodes
gives you that ability

00:43:48.620 --> 00:43:50.760
to standardize it and
create huge tables.

00:43:50.760 --> 00:43:53.810
And if you look up
potentials of reduction,

00:43:53.810 --> 00:43:56.900
they're all related to hydrogen.
You get thousands of them.

00:43:56.900 --> 00:44:02.012
How much does somebody want
to lose, how many electrons?

00:44:02.012 --> 00:44:03.470
That's going to be
in these tables.

00:44:03.470 --> 00:44:07.130
And it's by a setup that is very
similar to the one I showed you

00:44:07.130 --> 00:44:09.970
that is related to hydrogen. OK.

00:44:09.970 --> 00:44:11.990
Or you could go the other way.

00:44:11.990 --> 00:44:13.280
If I had zinc--

00:44:13.280 --> 00:44:15.980
I just showed you zinc.

00:44:15.980 --> 00:44:19.555
Copper in zinc doesn't happen
because it's the wrong sign.

00:44:19.555 --> 00:44:21.680
But then what if I put
copper-- instead of in zinc,

00:44:21.680 --> 00:44:22.700
I put copper in silver.

00:44:22.700 --> 00:44:24.710
So there's copper in
zinc, copper in silver.

00:44:24.710 --> 00:44:26.490
It works.

00:44:26.490 --> 00:44:30.747
Because now silver is down here.

00:44:30.747 --> 00:44:31.580
How did I know that?

00:44:31.580 --> 00:44:33.247
Because I hooked it
up to hydrogen and I

00:44:33.247 --> 00:44:35.960
compared it to copper.

00:44:35.960 --> 00:44:38.720
And so that's how you
do battery chemistry is

00:44:38.720 --> 00:44:39.790
you look things up.

00:44:39.790 --> 00:44:42.980
You look up these
potentials in tables,

00:44:42.980 --> 00:44:45.400
and you think about
which voltage you need.

00:44:45.400 --> 00:44:50.220
And that gets us to
this Nobel Prize.

00:44:50.220 --> 00:44:53.240
You say, well, why are lithium--

00:44:53.240 --> 00:44:56.620
why did the Nobel
Prize go to the--

00:44:56.620 --> 00:45:01.610
not discovery but the
development of lithium ions

00:45:01.610 --> 00:45:04.260
as a storage technology?

00:45:04.260 --> 00:45:07.490
And the reason is this.

00:45:07.490 --> 00:45:10.120
You saw-- it was the highest
one on the list, too.

00:45:10.120 --> 00:45:11.670
Look at that.

00:45:11.670 --> 00:45:14.035
So now, I don't know what
the potential of a lithium

00:45:14.035 --> 00:45:15.410
something battery
is going to be,

00:45:15.410 --> 00:45:19.400
but I'm starting out with
a pretty negative voltage

00:45:19.400 --> 00:45:21.110
with respect to
hydrogen. That makes

00:45:21.110 --> 00:45:23.515
you think you might get
high voltages, and you do.

00:45:23.515 --> 00:45:25.640
Yeah, but you have another
thing that's really cool

00:45:25.640 --> 00:45:27.620
about lithium is
how light it is.

00:45:27.620 --> 00:45:30.710
And those things combined
make it a beautiful battery

00:45:30.710 --> 00:45:31.730
technology.

00:45:31.730 --> 00:45:33.710
And that's why the
Nobel Prize was given.

00:45:33.710 --> 00:45:36.158
Because if you look at
it on a plot like this,

00:45:36.158 --> 00:45:38.450
it's all the way up here, if
you look at energy density

00:45:38.450 --> 00:45:39.860
versus volume.

00:45:39.860 --> 00:45:42.395
And a lithium battery works
much the same way in the sense

00:45:42.395 --> 00:45:43.770
that you have the
separator here.

00:45:43.770 --> 00:45:44.930
That's the electrolyte.

00:45:44.930 --> 00:45:47.610
But now it's rechargeable.

00:45:47.610 --> 00:45:50.982
And I don't have time to go
into rechargeable batteries

00:45:50.982 --> 00:45:52.940
versus what are called
primary batteries, which

00:45:52.940 --> 00:45:54.980
is the one I showed you.

00:45:54.980 --> 00:45:56.430
But all the chemistry
is the same.

00:45:56.430 --> 00:45:58.380
You're shuttling
ions back and forth.

00:45:58.380 --> 00:46:00.763
You've got that
external circuit.

00:46:00.763 --> 00:46:02.180
And what I wanted
to-- and there's

00:46:02.180 --> 00:46:03.330
a whole bunch of chemistry.

00:46:03.330 --> 00:46:05.330
So what has exploded
in the last--

00:46:05.330 --> 00:46:07.560
well, that's a bad
word for batteries

00:46:07.560 --> 00:46:09.560
because that is a danger
with lithium batteries.

00:46:09.560 --> 00:46:10.170
[LAUGHTER]

00:46:10.170 --> 00:46:13.070
But what has really
made the field take off

00:46:13.070 --> 00:46:15.710
is you know the word.

00:46:15.710 --> 00:46:16.580
We're in the class.

00:46:16.580 --> 00:46:18.110
It's chemistry.

00:46:18.110 --> 00:46:21.290
Because the flexibility
of where you

00:46:21.290 --> 00:46:24.440
can put that other electrode
and what properties

00:46:24.440 --> 00:46:26.450
it has gives you enormous room.

00:46:26.450 --> 00:46:28.985
That's why lithium-ion batteries
can go in everything now,

00:46:28.985 --> 00:46:30.110
all the way up to the grid.

00:46:30.110 --> 00:46:32.930
And that was my last
Why This Matters

00:46:32.930 --> 00:46:36.480
in the last 2 and 1/2 minutes.

00:46:36.480 --> 00:46:37.470
This is the grid now.

00:46:37.470 --> 00:46:40.920
So we went from
small to very big.

00:46:40.920 --> 00:46:44.910
The growth in solar and
wind has been incredible

00:46:44.910 --> 00:46:47.830
over the last 10, 20 years.

00:46:47.830 --> 00:46:51.220
The problem, as I think I've
shown you, is the variability.

00:46:51.220 --> 00:46:52.160
And so this is like--

00:46:52.160 --> 00:46:54.660
if you zoom in here, that's
the load, the black line.

00:46:54.660 --> 00:46:57.030
And this is what these
two resources give you.

00:46:57.030 --> 00:46:58.980
And you can see that
it doesn't match.

00:46:58.980 --> 00:47:03.010
If you zoom in on a day, then
you really can see it, right?

00:47:03.010 --> 00:47:03.910
There is the load.

00:47:03.910 --> 00:47:06.400
And there's what you would
get from solar and wind.

00:47:06.400 --> 00:47:07.550
And so here's the question.

00:47:07.550 --> 00:47:10.610
I said, well, OK,
how do you fix this?

00:47:10.610 --> 00:47:12.130
You got to store it.

00:47:12.130 --> 00:47:15.767
There's no way to use
renewables at large scales

00:47:15.767 --> 00:47:16.600
unless you store it.

00:47:16.600 --> 00:47:20.110
In fact, we're really at
essentially the tipping point.

00:47:20.110 --> 00:47:23.440
Because if I look at the
countries in the world and what

00:47:23.440 --> 00:47:25.940
their SPV penetration is-- and
this is a couple years old,

00:47:25.940 --> 00:47:27.482
but it's still pretty
much the same--

00:47:27.482 --> 00:47:30.640
Germany, Greece,
Italy at the top.

00:47:30.640 --> 00:47:34.060
Sad frowny face here
for the US of A.

00:47:34.060 --> 00:47:38.770
But Germany is at 7.1%
as of a few years ago.

00:47:38.770 --> 00:47:41.160
That's a huge penetration of PV.

00:47:41.160 --> 00:47:43.540
Well, here's the problem.

00:47:43.540 --> 00:47:46.010
If you look at--

00:47:46.010 --> 00:47:47.800
this is Germany, now.

00:47:47.800 --> 00:47:50.710
Huge penetration-- these
are these six years,

00:47:50.710 --> 00:47:52.810
seven years of adding PV.

00:47:52.810 --> 00:47:54.250
This is how much they had.

00:47:54.250 --> 00:47:55.720
They're now at 7%.

00:47:55.720 --> 00:47:57.070
2016 is 7%.

00:47:57.070 --> 00:48:00.400
This is how much the
price of electricity

00:48:00.400 --> 00:48:01.930
was in Germany when they added.

00:48:01.930 --> 00:48:03.790
So notice that here
is the sunlight

00:48:03.790 --> 00:48:05.380
electricity during the day.

00:48:05.380 --> 00:48:07.330
They can sell it
for a lot of money.

00:48:07.330 --> 00:48:10.840
But as they add more and more
of this renewable to the grid,

00:48:10.840 --> 00:48:13.690
its price goes down
when it's available.

00:48:13.690 --> 00:48:17.350
In fact, it's now not
economical for them

00:48:17.350 --> 00:48:21.660
to add more PV unless they
can add it with storage.

00:48:21.660 --> 00:48:23.730
Because actually the price
has gone down so much

00:48:23.730 --> 00:48:25.320
and it's gone up
here when they're not

00:48:25.320 --> 00:48:27.910
generating any PV electricity.

00:48:27.910 --> 00:48:30.690
So you can come up with--
and they did in this paper--

00:48:30.690 --> 00:48:33.330
this sort of value factor
which, when it goes below 1,

00:48:33.330 --> 00:48:37.860
means there's no economic
outcome that's positive here.

00:48:37.860 --> 00:48:40.850
You can't make money any more.

00:48:40.850 --> 00:48:42.150
And look at where that happens.

00:48:42.150 --> 00:48:43.525
It's right around
where they are.

00:48:43.525 --> 00:48:45.400
It's at 5%.

00:48:45.400 --> 00:48:48.460
You can't solve renewables
at the scale of the grid

00:48:48.460 --> 00:48:49.150
without storage.

00:48:49.150 --> 00:48:50.900
And no option exists today.

00:48:50.900 --> 00:48:52.353
It might be batteries.

00:48:52.353 --> 00:48:53.770
It might be
batteries, but there's

00:48:53.770 --> 00:48:56.500
nothing that actually does
it today at the scales

00:48:56.500 --> 00:48:57.050
that we need.

00:48:57.050 --> 00:49:00.310
So that's a real
challenge for chemistry.

00:49:00.310 --> 00:49:03.610
And I hope you guys have a great
Thanksgiving and maybe hook

00:49:03.610 --> 00:49:06.400
up some different metals.

00:49:06.400 --> 00:49:09.090
And I'll see you all next week.

